  "prompt": "Find the common rule that maps an input grid to an output grid, given the examples below. Generate a python script that takes a grid as input and transforms it according to the this rule. Anticipate that this program will be applied to several grids, not only the shown test grid.\n\nYour script must define exactly one callable entrypoint:\ndef solve(grid):\nwhere `grid` is passed as a list of lists of integers (list[list[int]]) and the function returns the transformed grid in the same format.\n\nExample 1:\n\nInput:\n0 8\n0 0\n0 8\nOutput:\n8 0 0 8\n0 0 0 0\n8 0 0 8\n8 0 0 8\n0 0 0 0\n8 0 0 8\n8 0 0 8\n0 0 0 0\n8 0 0 8\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput:\n2 0\n2 2\n2 0\nOutput:\n0 2 2 0\n2 2 2 2\n0 2 2 0\n0 2 2 0\n2 2 2 2\n0 2 2 0\n0 2 2 0\n2 2 2 2\n0 2 2 0\n\nExample 3:\n\nInput:\n0 0\n0 5\n5 0\nOutput:\n0 5 5 0\n5 0 0 5\n0 0 0 0\n0 0 0 0\n5 0 0 5\n0 5 5 0\n0 5 5 0\n5 0 0 5\n0 0 0 0\n\nBelow is a test input grid.\n\nInput:\n3 3\n3 0\n3 3\n\nYour final answer should just be the python script itself, no other text or markdown.",
  "raw_response": "def solve(grid):\n    # Transform each row into a palindrome: reverse(row) + row\n    transformed = [row[::-1] + row for row in grid]\n\n    n = len(grid)\n\n    if n == 1:\n        # Single row: repeat its palindrome 9 times\n        out = [transformed[0]] * 9\n    elif n == 2:\n        # Pattern: first row, second row, first row, repeated three times\n        pattern = [transformed[0], transformed[1], transformed[0]]\n        out = pattern * 3\n    else:\n        # For three or more rows, take the first three rows\n        rev_block = transformed[:3][::-1]          # reversed first three rows\n        orig_block = transformed[:3]               # original first three rows\n        out = rev_block + orig_block + rev_block   # 9 rows total\n\n    return out",
  "script": "def solve(grid):\n    # Transform each row into a palindrome: reverse(row) + row\n    transformed = [row[::-1] + row for row in grid]\n\n    n = len(grid)\n\n    if n == 1:\n        # Single row: repeat its palindrome 9 times\n        out = [transformed[0]] * 9\n    elif n == 2:\n        # Pattern: first row, second row, first row, repeated three times\n        pattern = [transformed[0], transformed[1], transformed[0]]\n        out = pattern * 3\n    else:\n        # For three or more rows, take the first three rows\n        rev_block = transformed[:3][::-1]          # reversed first three rows\n        orig_block = transformed[:3]               # original first three rows\n        out = rev_block + orig_block + rev_block   # 9 rows total\n\n    return out",
